cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you refer to this work, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Changing One Word: a paired test of anchor direction in list indexing"
version: "6.0"
date-released: 2026-10-27
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Kyssta"
    alias: "okyssta"
abstract: >-
  A paired midpoint analysis of anchor direction in list indexing: for odd-length lists, the
  backward and forward phrasings name the same item at the same ordinal, so only the anchor word
  differs. On Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct the backward wording reduces accuracy by 33-38 percentage
  points (95% CI excluding zero, not clearing a 15-point practical margin), and the estimated anchor
  effect differs between the two question templates tested (interaction -54.17 points, exact
  p = 2/4096). The direction asymmetry itself and prompt sensitivity in general are prior results.
  Small exploratory study: one model family, one position, 24 items per cell, uncorrected
  comparisons. No priority claim is made.
keywords:
  - large language models
  - positional retrieval
  - list indexing
  - prompt sensitivity
  - paired analysis
repository-code: "https://github.com/okyssta/llm-midpoint-anchor-study"
